PART D: IMPORTANT GIT COMMANDS
============================================================

1. Initialize a repository:
git init

2. Copy a remote repository:
git clone URL

3. Check file status:
git status

4. Add all changes to staging:
git add .

5. Commit staged changes:
git commit -m "message"

6. List branches:
git branch

7. Create and switch to a branch:
git switch -c branch-name

8. Switch to an existing branch:
git switch branch-name

9. Merge another branch into the current branch:
git merge branch-name

10. Upload commits:
git push

11. Download and integrate remote changes:
git pull

12. Download remote updates without merging:
git fetch

13. View commit history:
git log

14. View short commit history:
git log --oneline

15. Show remote repository addresses:
git remote -v

16. Connect a remote repository:
git remote add origin URL

17. Rename current branch to main:
git branch -M main

18. Push main branch and set upstream:
git push -u origin main

19. Unstage a file:
git restore --staged filename

20. Discard local changes in a file:
git restore filename

WARNING:
git restore filename discards uncommitted changes in that file. Use it carefully.

============================================================
PART E: IMPORTANT DIFFERENCES TO REMEMBER
============================================================

Git vs GitHub:
Git = version control tool.
GitHub = online hosting and collaboration platform.

git add vs git commit:
add = stage changes.
commit = save staged changes in history.

git fetch vs git pull:
fetch = download remote updates only.
pull = fetch and integrate remote updates.

git merge vs git rebase:
merge = combine branch histories.
rebase = replay commits on a new base.

Local vs remote repository:
local = on your computer.
remote = on a server such as GitHub.

Continuous Delivery vs Continuous Deployment:
Delivery = production release may need approval.
Deployment = production release is automatic after checks pass.

Docker vs Kubernetes:
Docker = builds and runs containers.
Kubernetes = manages and orchestrates containers.

